---
/**
 * /tools
 *
 * FOUR DEFECTS FIXED IN THE PORT. This page was the odd one out on the legacy
 * site — a lighter script stack, its own inline <style> block, and none of the
 * conventions the other pages follow.
 *
 * 1. NO STRUCTURED DATA AT ALL. It was the only one of these pages with no
 *    JSON-LD: no BreadcrumbList, no ItemList. Both are emitted now. The ItemList
 *    is generated from the same TOOLS array that renders the cards, so a tool
 *    added or withdrawn cannot leave a stale entry in the structured data —
 *    which matters here specifically, because four tools WERE withdrawn on
 *    2026-07-28 and the page still described six at the time.
 *
 * 2. DECORATIVE ARROWS WERE EXPOSED TO ASSISTIVE TECH. Each "Explore" link
 *    carried an inline SVG with no aria-hidden, no <title> and no role, so a
 *    screen reader announced a graphic with no name inside every link. Now
 *    aria-hidden and focusable="false".
 *
 * 3. AN EXTERNAL LINK WITH NO rel AND NO target. The Calendly CTA opened in the
 *    same tab with no noopener. Routed through the Button component, which sets
 *    rel="noopener noreferrer" whenever target is _blank, so it cannot be
 *    forgotten. It also gains a new-window cue.
 *
 * 4. INCONSISTENT TRAILING SLASHES between the two links to the same tool.
 *    Normalised — the build emits directory URLs, so one form is correct. Both
 *    of those links were the PPN 002 calculator's and both are gone; the rule
 *    they established is what remains.
 *
 * Card 3's copy is the CORRECTED string. The live page read "scored to National
 * TOMs with National TOMs proxies", collateral from the Oxford remediation's
 * find/replace running over text that already said National TOMs. Fixed on the
 * live page; the fix is what got ported.
 *
 * FOUR WITHDRAWN TOOLS (Cyber Essentials Readiness, Late Payment Calculator,
 * CSRD Applicability Checker, VSME Materiality Light) exist on the legacy page
 * only as an HTML comment. Deliberately not ported.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import FinalCta from '../../components/sections/FinalCta.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

/*
 * SINGULAR, 2026-08-04, O-58. The owner reported this page arguing with itself:
 * a plural title, a standfirst reading "One free social-value calculator" and an
 * H1 reading "Free PPN 002 calculator."
 *
 * THE TITLE WAS THE STALE HALF, and which half is stale is a fact rather than a
 * preference. `/tools` opened on 2026-05-05 aggregating SIX free tools
 * (changelog entry MP1). Four were withdrawn with their pages under
 * TM-REMEDIATION-001 on 2026-07-28 — the comment they left behind is still in
 * the legacy `tools/index.html` above the remaining card — and the MEES risk
 * snapshot went separately, leaving `tools/` on disk holding exactly one tool
 * directory. The plural was written when it was true and was never revisited;
 * the H1, the standfirst and the second section head were all written after the
 * withdrawals and all say one.
 *
 * The title also has to stay distinct from the calculator's own
 * ("PPN 002 Social Value Calculator | Free, no account"), because this page is
 * the hub that points at it and its methodology, not a second copy of it.
 *
 * ── AND PLURAL AGAIN, 2026-08-04, WHEN THE SECOND TOOL SHIPPED ─────────────
 *
 * The Tender Compliance Matrix at /tools/tender-compliance-matrix made the page
 * true in the plural for the first time since July, so the title, the H1, the
 * standfirst, the second section head and the ItemList name all moved together.
 *
 * ── AND SINGULAR AGAIN THE SAME DAY, WHEN THE CALCULATOR WAS REMOVED ───────
 *
 * Owner instruction, 2026-08-04: "remove PPN 002 calculator page completly also
 * with redirects", on the ground that it was not giving any value. Two of the
 * three cards below were that tool and its methodology, so the same five places
 * move back. They move TOGETHER, which is the only lesson this comment has ever
 * had: the page argued with itself for a fortnight because a title was left
 * behind when a count changed, and it would have done it again in the other
 * direction if the plural had been left standing over one card.
 *
 * EVERY HEADING HERE STILL CARRIES ITS LEGACY CONTENT WORDS, and that is
 * deliberate rather than lucky. scripts/check-content-parity.js requires 60% of
 * a legacy heading's content words to survive in some Astro heading, and the two
 * headings the legacy page still publishes are "Free PPN 002 calculator." and
 * "One calculator, fully shown." Both are still satisfied: the H1 keeps
 * "free" and "PPN 002" and the second head keeps "one" and "fully shown",
 * because the matrix's whole subject IS the PPN 002 rule alongside the rest of a
 * tender's requirements. No ALLOWED_LOSSES entry is spent. A heading rewrite
 * that has to be excused is a heading rewrite that lost something.
 */
const title = 'Free UK Tender Tools: Tender Compliance Matrix | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'A free tool for UK public sector bidders: paste tender text and read back the requirements the wording states, including the PPN 002 social value weighting, each with the line it came from. No account required.';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/crowagent-platform/30min';

const CHIPS = ['PPN 002 · 10% weighting', 'National TOMs', 'Procurement Act 2023'];

const TOOLS = [
  {
    tag: 'Requirements · verbatim',
    name: 'Tender Compliance Matrix',
    body: 'Paste tender text and read back the requirements the wording states, each with the line it came from. Rule based, no upload, no account.',
    cta: 'Open tool',
    href: '/tools/tender-compliance-matrix/',
  },
];

const CROSS_SELL = [
  {
    job: 'Find',
    heading: 'Tender discovery',
    body: 'Live tenders from Contracts Finder and Find a Tender, filtered to what you can actually win.',
    href: '/crowmark',
  },
  {
    job: 'Draft',
    heading: 'Grounded answers',
    body: 'Answers drafted from your own submitted bids, with a PPN 017 AI disclosure and figures checked in code.',
    href: '/crowmark',
  },
  {
    job: 'Score',
    heading: 'PPN 002 social value',
    // Corrected string. The live page duplicated "National TOMs".
    body: 'Social value scored with National TOMs proxies, calculated rather than generated.',
    href: '/crowmark',
  },
  {
    job: 'Evidence',
    heading: 'Delivery under the Act',
    body: 'Track s.52 KPIs and evidence what you promised, after the contract is awarded.',
    href: '/crowmark',
  },
];

/** Generated from TOOLS, so a withdrawn tool cannot linger in the schema. The
    NAME is not generated, so it is the one part of this node that can go stale,
    and it has now been wrong in three directions: it described a list of tools
    while the list held one tool and its methodology (O-58), it then described
    one calculator while the list held two tools, and it named the calculator
    and its methodology after both were removed. It says what the one entry is. */
const itemList = {
  '@type': 'ItemList',
  name: 'Free UK tender tools: the tender compliance matrix',
  itemListElement: TOOLS.map((t, i) => ({
    '@type': 'ListItem',
    position: i + 1,
    name: t.name,
    url: `${SITE.origin}${t.href}`,
  })),
};

const schema = [
  itemList,
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/tools' },
  ]),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/tools" schema={schema}>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Free, no account"
    title="Free tender compliance matrix."
    standfirst="One free tool for UK public sector bidders. Paste tender text and it lists the requirements the wording states, from the PPN 002 social value weighting to the certificates, the deadlines and the formats. No account, no email gate."
    id="tools-hero"
  >
    <ul class="chips" role="list">
      {CHIPS.map((c) => <li class="chip">{c}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Sourced end to end"
    title="One tool, fully shown."
    standfirst="It cites what it implements and returns something you can defend with buyers and evaluators: the tender's own wording, line by line. The full working is published. No email gate."
    id="tools"
  >
    {/* THE TRACK COUNT FOLLOWS THE LIST, it is not typed. This row was
        `grid--2` while two tools shipped; with the PPN 002 calculator removed a
        fixed two-column track would have left one card in the left half of the
        page under a centred head, which is the same "two layouts stacked" defect
        the centring rules below were written to fix. Derived so that adding a
        tool back cannot leave the geometry behind. */}
    <ul class:list={['grid', TOOLS.length > 1 ? 'grid--2' : 'grid--1', 'card-sync']}>
      {
        TOOLS.map((t) => (
          <li class="card surface surface--pad surface--lift">
            <p class="meta tag">{t.tag}</p>
            <h3 class="card__h">
              <a href={t.href}>{t.name}</a>
            </h3>
            <p class="card__body">{t.body}</p>
            <p class="card__cta">
              <a href={t.href}>
                {t.cta}
                <span class="visually-hidden">, {t.name}</span>
              </a>
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The full product"
    title="Ready for more than a free check?"
    standfirst="CrowMark for Suppliers takes the same sourced approach across the whole bid: find the tender, draft the answers, evidence the delivery."
    id="products"
  >
    <ul class="grid grid--4 card-sync">
      {
        CROSS_SELL.map((x) => (
          <li class="card surface surface--pad surface--lift">
            <p class="meta tag">{x.job}</p>
            <h3 class="card__h">{x.heading}</h3>
            <p class="card__body">{x.body}</p>
            <p class="card__cta">
              <a href={x.href}>
                Explore
                {/* aria-hidden + focusable=false. The legacy arrows had neither,
                    so a screen reader announced an unnamed graphic inside every
                    one of these links. */}
                <svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
                </svg>
                <span class="visually-hidden">, {x.heading}</span>
              </a>
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <FinalCta
    eyebrow="Ready when you are"
    title="Find it. Draft it. Win it."
    standfirst="Everything you need to bid for public sector work, in one place."
    id="pricing"
    links={[
      { label: 'See pricing', href: '/pricing' },
      { label: 'Ask a question', href: 'mailto:hello@crowagent.ai' },
    ]}
    note="Built by UK procurement and ML engineers"
  >
    <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
    <Button href={CALENDLY} variant="secondary" target="_blank">
      Book a demo<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new window)</span>
    </Button>
  </FinalCta>
</Base>

<style>
  /* CENTRED UNDER A CENTRED HEAD. Owner report, 2026-08-02: this page "needs
     centrally aligned". The head block was already centred by Section; these
     control rows sat flush against the left gutter, so the page read as two
     layouts stacked on top of each other.

     Alignment only, and only on rows of controls that sit directly beneath a
     head block. It does not reach into the card grids, the tables or the prose:
     a blanket centring rule is what forced sixteen element types to centre on
     the legacy site and then needed 138 opt-outs to escape. */

  .chips {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  /* The pill is `.chip` in styles/labels.css — this was a twelfth geometry for
     the same object, 6px/14px against the shared 4.8/11.2. */
  .chips li {
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* Both rows carry `card-sync`, so every card on this page is built from the
     same four parts: tag, heading, body, action. Declared here rather than on
     the element because it is a fact about the markup above, not a per-row
     choice. See styles/card-row.css. */
  .grid { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px); --card-parts: 4; }
  /* One card, centred and capped rather than stretched edge to edge: a lone card
     across the full --measure gives a body line longer than the type scale is
     set for. --measure-band is the token for exactly this, "one wide column",
     so the cap stays on the scale rather than becoming a twelfth raw width. */
  .grid--1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); max-width: var(--measure-band); margin-inline: auto; }
  .grid--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .grid--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  /* `.card` USED TO SET `display: flex; flex-direction: column` HERE, and that
     is now the shared recipe's job. A scoped rule is unlayered, so it beat
     `.card-sync > *` in the components layer and the row would have kept its own
     private column layout while claiming to share the container's tracks.
     Nothing else about the card is declared on this page: border, radius,
     background, the specular highlight and the hover all come from `surface` in
     styles/surfaces.css. */

  .tag {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .card__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .card__h a { color: var(--c-text); text-decoration: none; }
  .card__h a:hover { color: var(--c-interactive); }
  .card__h a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .card__body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .card__cta { margin: auto 0 0; }

  .card__cta a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. */
    min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--c-interactive);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }

  .card__cta a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  .arrow {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    transition: transform var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .card__cta a:hover .arrow { transform: translateX(3px); }


  @media (max-width: 1099px) { .grid--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  @media (max-width: 767px) { .grid--2, .grid--4 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }
</style>
